            <ArticleTitle>Acute scrotum caused by sigmoid diverticulitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>507-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1089/sur.2010.084</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Abscesses are one of many complications of diverticulitis and can be found intra-abdominally or in the pelvis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report and review of the pertinent English-language literature.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We describe an unusual presentation of a diverticular abscess, tracking down the inguinal canal and causing acute scrotum possibly secondary to a hernia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A deviation from the usual clinical presentation of diverticulitis should prompt the physician to obtain further radiologic evaluation. Computed tomography scanning is recommended, especially when an abscess is suspected.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
